"CSS Properties and Values API Level 1 (https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/)\nciteturn1264view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/#calculation\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1339\nL0: # CSS Properties and Values API Level 1\nL1: \nL2: cite0†Editor’s Draft†www.w3.org , 6 August 2026\nL3: \nL4: More details about this document\nL5: This version:\nL6:     cite1†https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/ L7: Latest published version:\nL8:     cite2†https://www.w3.org/TR/css-properties-values-api-1/†www.w3.org L9: Previous Versions:\nL10:     cite3†https://www.w3.org/TR/2020/WD-css-properties-values-api-1-20201013/†www.w3.org L11:     cite4†https://www.w3.org/TR/2019/WD-css-properties-values-api-1-20191025/†www.w3.org L12:     cite5†https://www.w3.org/TR/2017/WD-css-properties-values-api-1-20171109/†www.w3.org L13:     cite6†https://www.w3.org/TR/2016/WD-css-properties-values-api-1-20160607/†www.w3.org L14: Feedback:\nL15:     public-houdini@w3.org with subject line “`[css-properties-values-api] … message topic …`” (cite7†archives†lists.w3.org )\nL16:     cite8†GitHub†github.com L17:     cite9†Inline In Spec L18: Editors:\nL19:     cite10†Tab Atkins-Bittner†xanthir.com (Google)\nL20:     Alan Stearns (stearns@adobe.com)\nL21:     Greg Whitworth (gwhitworth@salesforce.com)\nL22: Former Editors:\nL23:     Shane Stephens (shanestephens@google.com)\nL24:     Daniel Glazman (daniel.glazman@disruptive-innovations.com)\nL25:     Elliot Sprehn (esprehn@chromium.org)\nL26: cite11†Copyright†www.w3.org © 2026 cite12†World Wide Web Consortium†www.w3.org . W3C (World Wide Web Consortium)^{®} cite13†liability†www.w3.org , cite14†trademark†www.w3.org and cite15†permissive document license†www.w3.org rules apply.\nL27: \nL28: * * *\nL29: ## Abstract\nL30: \nL31: This CSS module defines an API for registering new CSS properties. Properties registered using this API are provided with a parse syntax that defines a type, inheritance behaviour, and an initial value.\nL32: ## Status of this document\nL33: \nL34: This is a public copy of the editors’ draft. It is provided for discussion only and may change at any moment. Its publication here does not imply endorsement of its contents by W3C. Don’t cite this document other than as work in progress.\nL35: Please send feedback by cite16†filing issues in GitHub†github.com (preferred), including the spec code “css-properties-values-api” in the title, like this: “[css-properties-values-api] …summary of comment…”. All issues and comments are cite17†archived†lists.w3.org . Alternately, feedback can be sent to the (cite18†archived†lists.w3.org ) public mailing list www-style@w3.org.\nL36: \nL37: This document is governed by the cite19†18 August 2025 W3C Process Document†www.w3.org .\nL38: ## Table of Contents\nL39:   1. cite20†1 Introduction L40:   2. cite21†2 Registered Custom Properties L41:     1. cite22†2.1 Determining the Registration L42:     2. cite23†2.2 Parse-Time Behavior L43:     3. cite24†2.3 Specified Value-Time Behavior L44:     4. cite25†2.4 Computed Value-Time Behavior L45:     5. cite26†2.5 Animation Behavior L46:     6. cite27†2.6 Conditional Rules L47:     7. cite28†2.7 Substitution via var() L48:       1. cite29†2.7.1 Dependency Cycles via Relative Units L49:     8. cite30†2.8 Shadow DOM L50:   3. cite31†3 The @property Rule L51:     1. cite32†3.1 The syntax Descriptor L52:     2. cite33†3.2 The inherits Descriptor L53:     3. cite34†3.3 The initial-value Descriptor L54:   4. cite35†4 Registering Custom Properties in JS L55:     1. cite36†4.1 The `registerProperty()` Function L56:     2. cite37†4.2 The `PropertyDefinition` Dictionary L57:   5. cite38†5 Syntax Strings L58:     1. cite39†5.1 Supported Names L59:     2. cite40†5.2 The '+' and '#' Multipliers L60:     3. cite41†5.3 The '|' Combinator L61:     4. cite42†5.4 Parsing The Syntax String L62:       1. cite43†5.4.1 Definitions L63:       2. cite44†5.4.2 Consume a Syntax Definition L64:       3. cite45†5.4.3 Consume a Syntax Component L65:       4. cite46†5.4.4 Consume a Data Type Name L66:   6. cite47†6 CSSOM L67:     1. cite48†6.1 The `CSSPropertyRule` Interface L68:     2. cite49†6.2 `CSSStyleValue` Reification L69:   7. cite50†7 Examples L70:     1. cite51†7.1 Example 1: Using custom properties to add animation behavior L71:     2. cite52†7.2 Example 2: Using @property to register a property L72:   8. cite53†8 Security Considerations L73:   9. cite54†9 Privacy Considerations L74:   10. cite55†10 Changes L75:     1. cite56†10.1 Changes since the Working Draft of 13 October 2020 L76:   11. cite57†Conformance L77:     1. cite58†Document conventions L78:     2. cite59†Conformance classes L79:     3. cite60†Partial implementations L80:       1. cite61†Implementations of Unstable and Proprietary Features L81:     4. cite62†Non-experimental implementations L82:   12. cite63†Index L83:     1. cite64†Terms defined by this specification L84:     2. cite65†Terms defined by reference L85:   13. cite66†References L86:     1. cite67†Normative References L87:     2. cite68†Non-Normative References L88:   14. cite69†Property Index L89:     1. cite70†@property Descriptors L90:   15. cite71†IDL Index L91:   16. cite9†Issues Index L92: ## 1. Introduction\nL93: \nL94: CSS defines a comprehensive set of properties that can be manipulated in order to modify the layout, paint, or behaviour of a web document. However, web authors frequently wish to extend this set with additional properties.\nL95: cite72†[css-variables] provides primitive means for defining user-controlled properties, however these properties always take token lists as values, must always inherit, and can only impact document layout or paint by being re-incorporated into the value of other properties via a var() reference.\nL96: \nL97: This specification extends cite72†[css-variables] , allowing the registration of properties that have a value type, an initial value, and a defined inheritance behaviour, via two methods:\nL98:   * A JS API, the `cite73†registerProperty() ` method\nL99: \nL100:   * A CSS at-rule, the cite74†@property rule\nL101: \nL102: This specification is complementary to cite75†[css-paint-api-1] and cite76†[css-layout-api-1] , which allow custom properties to directly impact paint and layout behaviours respectively.\nL103: ## 2. Registered Custom Properties\nL104: \nL105: A cite77†custom property†drafts.csswg.org can become a registered custom property, making it act more like a UA-defined property: giving it a syntax that’s checked by the UA, an initial value, and a specific inheritance behavior. This can be done by the cite74†@property rule, or the `cite73†registerProperty() ` JS function.\nL106: A cite77†custom property†drafts.csswg.org is considered to be registered for a `cite78†Document†dom.spec.whatwg.org ` if there is a valid cite74†@property rule defined for its name in one of the document’s stylesheets, or its name is cite79†contained†infra.spec.whatwg.org in the document’s `cite80†[[registeredPropertySet]] ` slot (that is, `cite73†registerProperty() ` was called to register it).\nL107: A cite81†registered custom property acts similarly to an unregistered cite77†custom property†drafts.csswg.org , except as defined below.\nL108: ### 2.1. Determining the Registration\nL109: \nL110: A cite81†registered custom property has a custom property registration that contains all the data necessary to treat it like a real property. It’s a cite82†struct†infra.spec.whatwg.org consisting of:\nL111: \nL112:   * a property name (a cite83†custom property name string )\nL113: \nL114:   * optionally, a syntax (a cite84†syntax string ) (defaulting to `\"*\"`)\nL115: \nL116:   * optionally, an inherit flag (a cite85†boolean†infra.spec.whatwg.org ) (defaulting to `true`)\nL117:   * optionally, an initial value (a CSS value which successfully cite86†parses†drafts.csswg.org according to the syntax) (defaulting to the cite87†guaranteed-invalid value†drafts.csswg.org )\nL118: \nL119: If the `cite78†Document†dom.spec.whatwg.org `’s `cite80†[[registeredPropertySet]] ` slot cite88†contains†infra.spec.whatwg.org a record with the cite77†custom property’s†drafts.csswg.org name, the registration is that record.\nL120: Otherwise, if the `cite78†Document†dom.spec.whatwg.org `’s active stylesheets contain at least one valid cite74†@property rule representing a registration with the cite77†custom property’s†drafts.csswg.org name, the last such one in document order is the registration.\nL121: \nL122: Otherwise there is no registration, and the cite77†custom property†drafts.csswg.org is not a cite81†registered custom property .\nL123: ### 2.2. Parse-Time Behavior\nL124: \nL125: cite81†Registered custom properties parse exactly like unregistered cite77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org ; almost anything is allowed. The registered syntax of the property is not checked at parse time.\nL126: \nL127: Note: However, the syntax is checked at computed-value time, before substitution via cite89†var()†drafts.csswg.org . See cite25†§ 2.4 Computed Value-Time Behavior .\nL128: \nL129: Why aren’t custom properties syntax-checked?\nL130: When parsing a page’s CSS, UAs commonly make a number of optimizations to help with both speed and memory.\nL131: \nL132: One of those optimizations is that they only store the properties that will actually have an effect; they throw away invalid properties, and if you write the same property multiple times in a single declaration block, all but the last valid one will be thrown away. (This is an important part of CSS’s error-recovery and forward-compatibility behavior.)\nL133: This works fine if the syntax of a property never changes over the lifetime of a page. If a custom property is registered, however, it can change its syntax, so that a property that was previously invalid suddenly becomes valid.\nL134: The only ways to handle this are to either store every declaration, even those that were initially invalid (increasing the memory cost of pages), or to re-parse the entire page’s CSS with the new syntax rules (increasing the processing cost of registering a custom property). Neither of these are very desirable.\nL135: Further, UA-defined properties have their syntax determined by the version of the UA the user is viewing the page with; this is out of the page author’s control, which is the entire reason for CSS’s error-recovery behavior and the practice of writing multiple declarations for varying levels of support. A custom property, on the other hand, has its syntax controlled by the page author, according to whatever stylesheet or script they’ve included in the page; there’s no unpredictability to be managed.\nL136: Throwing away syntax-violating custom properties would thus only be, at best, a convenience for the page author, not a necessity like for UA-defined properties.\nL137: ### 2.3. cite90†Specified Value†drafts.csswg.org -Time Behavior\nL138: \nL139: Just like unregistered cite77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org , all cite81†registered custom properties , regardless of registered syntax, accept the cite91†CSS-wide keywords†drafts.csswg.org , such as cite92†inherit†drafts.csswg.org or cite93†revert†drafts.csswg.org . Their behavior is defined in cite94†CSS Cascading 4 § 7.3 Explicit Defaulting†drafts.csswg.org .\nL140: ### 2.4. cite95†Computed Value†drafts.csswg.org -Time Behavior\nL141: \nL142: The cite95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org of a cite81†registered custom property is determined by the syntax of its cite96†registration .\nL143: \nL144: If the cite96†registration’s syntax is the cite97†universal syntax definition , the cite95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org is the same as for unregistered cite77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org (either the specified value with variables substituted, or the cite87†guaranteed-invalid value†drafts.csswg.org ).\nL145: Otherwise, attempt to cite86†parse†drafts.csswg.org the property’s value according to its registered syntax. If this fails, the declaration is cite98†invalid at computed-value time†drafts.csswg.org and the cite95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org is determined accordingly. If it succeeds, the cite95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org depends on the specifics of the syntax:\nL146: \nL147: For `\"<length>\"`, `\"<length-percentage>\"`, `\"<angle>\"`, `\"<time>\"`, `\"<resolution>\"`, `\"<integer>\"`, `\"<number>\"`, and `\"<percentage>\"` values:\nL148:   * If the specified value is a cite99†dimension†drafts.csswg.org literal (such as 50em or .2s), the computed value is the same value, but with the unit converted to the corresponding cite100†canonical unit†drafts.csswg.org for the type of value.\nL149: \nL150:   * If the specified value is any other numeric literal (such as 5 or 20%), the computed value is as specified. (In particular, percentages are never resolved against anything.)\nL151:   * If the specified value is a function that evaluates to one of those types (such as a cite101†math function†drafts.csswg.org ), the computed value is defined by that function.\nL152: \nL153: For `\"<string>\"` values, the computed value is as specified.\nL154: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeb Animations Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/)\nciteturn1264view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/#computing-property-values\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 4503\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn1264view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/#attr-substitution\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2842\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Cascading and Inheritance Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-cascade-5/)\nciteturn1264view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-cascade-5/#defaulting\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1419\n"
